course: everyday
lesson: 301
title: Tsunami warnings, lambs, berries, thorns, cacti, fire ants and bee stings
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Nature gives warnings, and part of being safe outdoors is reading them: a long, strong earthquake near the sea, a hedge of unknown berries, a cactus, an ant hill, a field of lambs.
explain: An earthquake under the sea can push up a huge wave called a tsunami, which may reach the coast within minutes. In New Zealand children learn "Long or strong, get gone": if the shaking is long or strong, go to high ground or inland at once, without waiting for a siren.
explain: Many berries look alike, and some are poisonous. Pick only berries a grown-up has identified, from above dog height and away from roads, and wash them before eating.
explain: Example: a girl in Queensland sits down on a sandy mound for a picnic. It is a fire ant nest, and within seconds many ants sting her again and again. Now she always checks the ground before she sits.
explain: A honeybee's sting has tiny barbs, like a fish hook, so it gets stuck in our skin and the bee usually dies. Wasps and bumblebees have smooth stings and can sting again, so "every bee dies when it stings" is a myth.
fact: If the sea suddenly pulls far back from the beach, a tsunami may be coming: go to high ground at once.
fact: If an earthquake at the coast is long or strong, go to high ground or inland at once, because a tsunami may follow within minutes.
fact: In spring, look at lambs but keep away from them and keep dogs on a lead, because mother sheep may panic and dogs can chase and hurt sheep.
fact: Many berries look alike and some are poisonous; pick only berries a grown-up has identified.
fact: Pick berries above dog height and away from roads, because low ones may be dirty from dogs and roadside ones polluted by traffic fumes, and wash them before eating.
fact: Move carefully near thorny bushes such as roses and brambles, and get a grown-up's help to pull out a thorn.
fact: Look at cacti but do not touch them; their spines go into skin and are hard to get out.
fact: Keep away from ant hills and never sit on them; fire ants in parts of the USA and Australia sting many times.
fact: Speak quietly and leave music at home in nature, because noise frightens animals and disturbs other visitors.
fact: A honeybee's sting is barbed and gets stuck in skin, so the bee usually dies after stinging a person.
fact: Wasps and bumblebees do not usually die when they sting and can sting more than once.
exercise: You are on a beach in New Zealand when the ground shakes hard for almost a minute. What should you do? | Go to high ground or inland at once | Wait on the beach until a siren sounds | Go down to the water to watch the sea | Stay where you are until it stops for an hour
exercise: Why should you leave the coast after a long or strong earthquake? | A tsunami may follow within minutes | The sand on the beach becomes too hot | The tide always goes out for a whole day | Seagulls get angry after earthquakes
exercise: You walk past a field full of new lambs with your dog. What is right? | Keep your dog on a lead and watch the lambs from a distance | Let your dog off the lead to run in and say hello to the lambs | Climb in to stroke the lambs | Call the lambs over with some bread
exercise: Why should dogs be on a lead near sheep? | Dogs can chase and hurt sheep | Sheep chase and bite dogs | Lambs are afraid only of cats | Dogs catch colds from sheep
exercise: You find a hedge full of dark berries. Which ones can you pick? | Only ones a grown-up has identified, high up and away from roads | Any berry that looks shiny, dark and ripe, wherever it grows | Any berry that birds are eating | The ones lowest down, easiest to reach
exercise: Why pick berries above dog height and away from roads? | Lower berries may be dirty and roadside ones polluted | Lower berries are always poisonous | High berries are always the sweetest, because they get the most sun | Roadside berries are owned by the council
exercise: A bramble thorn is stuck in your finger. What should you do? | Get a grown-up to help pull it out | Leave it in; it will fall out by itself | Bite it out carefully with your teeth | Push it in deeper so that it goes away
exercise: You see a fluffy-looking cactus in a garden in Arizona. What should you do? | Look but do not touch it | Stroke it gently, since it looks soft | Touch only the tip of one spine | Pick a piece to take home
exercise: Why is touching a cactus a bad idea? | Its spines go into skin and are hard to get out | Cactus juice burns the skin like fire for days | Cacti bite if you touch them | Cacti shrink when touched
exercise: You find a sandy mound in a park in Texas and want to sit down. What should you do? | Check for ants and never sit on an ant hill | Sit down on it; ants only bite at night when it's cool | Sit on it to squash the ants | Kick it to see what lives inside
exercise: What makes fire ants dangerous? | They sting many times | They spit burning fire at you | They only sting once and die | They carry away your food
exercise: Your friend wants to play loud music on a forest walk. What do you say? | Let's keep it quiet so we don't frighten animals | Louder music keeps the animals happy and helps them find us | Music is fine if we sing along | Animals cannot hear music
exercise: Which is true about stings? | Honeybees usually die after stinging a person, but wasps do not | Every bee and every wasp dies straight after it stings someone | Wasps die but honeybees survive | Bumblebees always die after one sting
exercise: Why does a honeybee usually die after stinging a person? | Its barbed sting gets stuck in the skin | It runs out of poison for ever | It is squashed by the person every time | It is frightened to death
test: A boy in Oregon feels a long, rolling earthquake while on the beach. No siren sounds. What should he do? | Go to high ground at once without waiting | Wait for the siren to sound before he moves off the beach | Watch the sea to see if it changes | Go home only if the shaking comes back
test: What does "Long or strong, get gone" mean? | After a long or strong earthquake near the coast, leave for high ground | Long walks on the beach make you strong and healthy | Leave a party when it gets loud | Run away from strong winds
test: A family walks through a farm in Yorkshire in April. Their dog pulls towards the lambs. What is right? | Keep the dog on a short lead and walk past calmly | Let the dog off the lead to run and play with the lambs | Let the children chase the lambs | Throw sticks for the dog in the field
test: Two berries look almost the same. One is safe and one is poisonous. What should you do? | Eat neither unless a grown-up who knows identifies them | Eat the darker one | Taste a tiny bit of each | Eat the one that has more berries growing on the bush
test: Which is the best way to deal with a thorn in your knee? | Ask a grown-up to take it out carefully | Ignore it for a week | Rub dirt on it | Squeeze it hard with dirty fingers until it pops out
test: A girl in Brisbane sees ants swarming out of a mound. Why should she step away? | Fire ants can sting many times | Ants spray water at people who come close | Ants only want to be friends and say hello | The mound might be a tiny volcano
test: Why do park rangers ask visitors to keep noise down? | Noise frightens animals and disturbs other visitors | Noise makes the trees and flowers grow much more slowly | Rangers need to sleep | Sound makes the rivers flood
test: A wasp stings a boy and flies off. His friend says, "Don't worry, it will die now." What is true? | Wasps do not usually die after stinging and can sting again | All stinging insects die at once, so it can never sting again | Wasps die but only after an hour | The wasp will lose its wings
test: big long quake at the beach, it's stopped now. what next | Go to high ground or inland straight away | Stay on the beach to watch the sea | Go home and wait for news | Walk out to see where the water went and look for shells
test: found red berries on the trail, they look yummy, eat? | No, only eat berries a grown-up has identified | Yes, red berries are always safe to eat, they're like strawberries | Yes, if birds eat them too | Eat one to test it first
test: my bro says bees die after they sting, wasps too? | Honeybees usually do, wasps can sting again | Yes, all stinging insects die | No, only wasps die after stinging | No, no insect ever dies from stinging, that's just a myth
test: wanna touch the cactus, looks soft | Don't, the spines go in and are hard to get out | Fine, cactus spines are soft like fur on that kind | Fine, if you touch it quickly | Only touch the flower part
explain: In 2004 a girl on holiday in Thailand saw the sea suddenly pull far back; she remembered a school lesson about tsunamis, warned her family and the beach was cleared before the wave came.
explain: A boy in Scotland ate shiny red berries from a bush in the park; he was sick and dizzy, and his mum had to rush him to hospital.
fact: Many berries that look like food are poisonous, and young children are poisoned by them every year.
fact: A bee sting can cause a dangerous allergic reaction in some people, with swelling and trouble breathing; if that happens, call the emergency number at once.
test: these red berries look like cranberries, can i eat em | Only eat berries an adult has checked | Yes, if they taste sweet | Yes, if birds eat them | Yes, if they're washed
test: friend got stung and now his face is puffing up and hes wheezy | Call the emergency number now | Put cool mud on it and wait for it to pass | Give him some sweets to calm him down | Tell him to run it off around the park
test: sea went way out after the shake, can we look for shells | No, go to high ground now, a tsunami may come | Yes, the shells are best when the sea is out | Yes, but stay near the edge | Wait and watch the water first
test: did u hear a little kid ate berries off a bush at the park and "had to get his tummy pumped". lesson? | Pick only berries a grown-up has identified | Red berries are always safe | Birds eat them so they're fine for us too, that's how you tell | Just wash them first and they're safe
test: my cousin says a girl sat on an ant hill in Texas and "got stung 500 times, her whole body went red". what's prob exaggerated | The exact number; still, fire ants sting many times, so keep away | That fire ants sting | That big ant hills exist in Texas | That stings can hurt
test: kids say the boy whose dog chased the lambs is "a sheep killer". pass it on? | No, it's a cruel rumour; just keep dogs on a lead near lambs | Yes, he's a sheep killer | Yes, and tell the farmer his name and where he lives so he gets told off | Yes, it's true
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the conduct required following a long or strong earthquake at the coast? | Persons shall go to high ground or inland at once | Persons shall go to the beach to observe the sea | Persons shall remain at home until the next day | Persons shall wait for the water to recede
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a honeybee usually dies after stinging a person. | Its barbed sting becomes stuck in the skin | It uses all its venom in one sting | It is crushed by the person in every case, whether struck or not | It cannot fly after it has stung
exam: In respect of cacti, select the response consistent with established guidance. | They shall be looked at but not touched, since spines are hard to remove | They may be touched gently upon the spines, which are soft when young | They may be hugged if small | They shall be squeezed to release water
exam: State the reason for which a bee sting may prove dangerous to certain persons. | It may cause a severe allergic reaction with swelling and difficult breathing | It always causes a high fever lasting several days | It causes the person to become a beekeeper | It is invariably fatal to everyone
